(Siem Reap): Autumn is upon the rice fields, which have changed from green to golden brown in the beautiful Angkor resort. Farmers are also busy harvesting rice, although they are tired but their faces are full of smiles. .
Nowadays, the technology in the agricultural sector has become very modern, and although farmers have changed their habits from using human and livestock to using machines to grow crops, the villagers in the Angkor area continue to maintain the tradition of hand-harvesting. Traditionally, it has been around for a long time, and this hand-to-hand measurement also involves the removal of seedlings, plowing, harrowing, and transportation.
Hand-harvesting shows solidarity in a community that knows how to help each other, costs less, is happier, and harvests better than using machines.
The APSARA Authority expresses its appreciation and support for maintaining the unity of the harvest, the use of ancient tools, as well as the traditions of the people of Angkor, as well as the rituals and beliefs to live on today. .
