Heads of government of member states as well as representatives of the European Union regularly meet at the G7 annual summit, which is held at regular intervals. As of 2018, the G7 is estimated to represent 58% of the world's total assets or more than 46% of the world's GDP.
Russia was originally a member of the G8, but the Russian Federation was ousted by former US President Barack Obama along with other members of the G8 in 2014. The government of Moscow occupied Crimea from Ukraine.
According to the European Union (EU) website, the G7, originally launched in 1975, is an informal platform to bring together leaders of the world's leading industrialized nations. The G7 annual summit has been developed over the years as a platform for setting the direction of multilateral dialogue and politics in response to global issues.
The G7 annual meeting has the potential to set the world agenda because the decisions made by these major economic powers have a real impact. The political direction set by these leaders on policy issues will have an "impact" throughout international organizations and institutions. So the decision at the G7 is not legitimate, but it has strong political influence.
During the Kovid-19 outbreak, the 2020 annual meeting hosted by the United States, Donald Trump, expressed a desire to see four more countries, including India, Australia, South Korea and Russia, return to the G7. Also. He claimed that this was to solve the problem of Kovid-19, which is a global problem, not just for the G7.
