2: Structure
The European Parliament The European Parliament is the highest body in the European Union's governing system, representing the will of the people of Europe as a whole. The membership of this parliament has increased with the growth of its people from one term to another. All members are directly elected by the people of Europe every five years.
The European Parliament has the following important functions: - To oversee and approve EU laws or budgets, of which the European Parliament has equal rights with the Council on joint decisions. - To conduct democratic oversight of other institutions. Of the European Union, which has the power to establish a commission of inquiry - to decide on international agreements as well as to add new members.
* European Council
The Council of Europe is an institution sometimes referred to as the Council of Ministers of the European Union, which has half the powers of the member states, half of which is the legislature and the other half is the executive branch of the European Union. The council consists of members from all EU member states that go through national elections.
The Council is also a forum for member states to protect their interests through representation and to draft international agreements or treaties. These government representatives shall meet regularly as a working group of ambassadors or ambassadors, ministers, or as heads of state or government when they make decisions on policies or guidelines within the framework of European Union.
The European Commission The European Commission is the supreme body of the European Union, which has the executive power as a whole and has a lot of work to do, such as drafting laws and proposing laws or agreements to the European Parliament and the Council of Europe for decision. The Committee is responsible for overseeing the actual implementation and overall expenditure of the EU as a whole.
In addition, the Committee is a key body that has the power to oversee whether member states or new laws or newly established treaties adhere to the common principles of the EU legal system.
The European Commission, headquartered in Brussels, Belgium, has 20 members, elected by member states, and has sought the approval of the European Parliament for a five-year term.
* Economic and Social Commission The Commission is a special body responsible for protecting and promoting the economic and social interests of all EU member states, especially stakeholders such as investors, employers, workers, unions and parliaments. Businesses and consumers.
As a consulting and consulting body to disseminate information related to key aspects of the concept, important role for cooperation and unity, to promote this work to double the momentum and efficiency.
European Central Bank The European Central Bank (ECB) is responsible for managing and controlling the European Union (EU) common currency, which was adopted in 1999 by 11 countries: Belgium, Spain, France, Germany, Italy, Italy, Finland, Luxembourg and the Netherlands. Austria and Portugal.
The Bank is headquartered in Frankfurt, Germany and has the authority to decide automatically and independently of EU monetary policy as a whole, such as exchange rate and interest rates.
* The European Investment Bank is an international monetary institution that provides foreign lending services to all EU member states in order to promote the investment and economy of all member states without widening the development gap.
Most of the loan projects the bank provides are for the construction of railways, national highways, airways, the environment and a number of partner banks to boost employment and the economy. The European Investment Bank is headquartered in Luxembourg.
* The European Court of Justice is a regional international judicial body that has the primary role of governing and monitoring violations of laws or regulations or treaties that the EU has already decided, consisting of 15 independent judges and Headquartered in Luxembourg.
It has the authority to resolve legal disputes that have already been approved by the European Union through requests from national courts when uncertainties arise. Not only that, individuals or individuals in the EU can also file a complaint to this court as long as the subject of the lawsuit is relevant to EU institutions.
* The European Court of Audit is a court that has jurisdiction over the management and control over the expenditure of EU funds. The tribunal has the authority and authority to audit all EU institutions to ensure the cleanliness and proper use of EU and other lower-level institutions.
