New Zealand Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern has stressed that the declaration of a climate emergency is based on co-operation with intergovernmental groups on climate change to prevent global warming above 1.5 degrees Celsius. And CO2 emissions, which will need to be reduced to about 45 percent from 2010 to 2023 and to zero by 2050.
"The declaration of a climate emergency is a recognition for future generations," Ardern said. "It's acknowledging the burden they will have to take if we do not take action now."
New Zealand has joined 32 other countries, including Japan, Canada, France and the United Kingdom, in declaring a state of emergency.
