In Cambodia today, there are between 400 and 600 Asian elephants, most of which live in the Cardamom Mountains, the northern plateau of the Tonle Sap and the eastern highlands of Mondulkiri, which are rich in biodiversity and ecosystems. The slope is conducive to the survival of elephants and other wildlife. The number of domestic elephants is estimated at 71.
Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary covers an area of nearly 300,000 hectares, covering four communes in Keo Seima District, Mondulkiri Province. The wildlife sanctuary has been identified by researchers as having high potential for future eco-tourism development.
