Cambodia-Korea demonstrates community forestry carbon production in Kampong Thom province on nearly 70,000 hectares
(Phnom Penh): To help prevent and reduce forest loss and degradation in Cambodia, the Forestry Administration signed a Memorandum of Understanding with the Republic of Korea through the Korea Forest Service on December 10, 2014 in a joint project initiative. The so-called "Red Plus Korea Korea Project" or the Red Plus Cambodia project, which is implemented in 14 community forests in Sandan and Santuk districts of Kampong Thom province, covers an area of 67,791.17 hectares.
The Red Plus project has reduced greenhouse gas emissions by 645,410 tons, equivalent to CO2, during the verification period from January 2015 to December 2019. These loans will be sold on the voluntary carbon market, and the proceeds from the sale of the loan will be used for sustainable forest management, improving the livelihoods of community forestry members in the project area, and supporting the sustainability of project implementation.
Kiyeon Ko, Director General of the Korea Forest Service, said: "We are pleased that the REDD + project has succeeded in generating carbon credits for community forestry through strong cooperation between the Korean Forest Service and the Forestry Administration. The production of this carbon credit shows that the forests in the project area are well managed and protected.
