Indian Embassy Distributes Relief to Flood Victims in Kampong Chhnang and Battambang Provinces
(Phnom Penh): The Embassy of the Republic of India in Cambodia, together with the Association of Indians in Cambodia, Indian Businessmen in Cambodia and the Association of Cambodian Alumni to Study in India, donated rice and some medicine to the flood victims in Cambodia. In Kampong Chhnang and Battambang provinces.
Ms. Tevyni Cobragade expressed her deepest condolences to the flood victims and welcomed the participation of the Indian community, Indian businessmen and members of the Cambodian Alumni Association of India in donating rice to villagers in Kampong Chhnang province.
Secretary of State Sao Chivoan thanked for the donations provided by the Indian Embassy and assured of facilitating the distribution of emergency relief programs in other affected provinces of Cambodia.
On the same day, the above-mentioned Cambodian-Indian philanthropist group continued to travel to Battambang province to distribute rice and other medicines to the villagers in Sampov Loun district. Prior to the humanitarian work, the Ambassador met with Battambang Provincial Governor Nguon Rattanak and handed over some medicines for use in a hospital in Battambang, as these drugs were donated by Indian pharmaceutical companies in Cambodia.
During the meeting, Mr. Nguon Ratanak said that this year, Battambang province was affected more than previous years due to the flood in 13 communes with more than 70,000 families affected the most. He also provided details on the Cambodian government's relief measures.
The Indian Ambassador expressed solidarity with the Cambodian people and expressed hope that the relief items, including rice, medicine, anti-Kovid hygiene kits donated by the Indian community, Sonalika Pharmaceuticals and other business groups, would be able to help the people. Cambodians on the road to recovery.
Ms. Tevyni Cobragade also mentioned various projects of the Government of India for the development of Battambang and other provinces such as irrigation and water source construction, installation of 1,500 pump wells.
The district authorities highlighted the seriousness of the loss and the hardships of the villagers in the six communes of Sampov Loun district and praised the assistance provided by the Indians.
