Source: Vox Legal Awareness: Forced Physical
(Phnom Penh): In principle, when a criminal offense occurs, two lawsuits will arise at the same time: the prosecutor's lawsuit and the civil lawsuit, which are two separate lawsuits. The purpose of the prosecution is to examine the existence of the offense, prove the guilt of the perpetrator and punish him in accordance with the law (penalties and additional penalties).
Meanwhile, the civil lawsuit aims to repair the damage caused by the crime to the victim and for this purpose is for the victim to receive adequate compensation for the damage he received.
Pursuant to the provisions of Articles 523 to 533, the Code of Criminal Procedure of the Kingdom of Cambodia provides for procedures for the implementation of coercive measures against prisoners.
Physical coercion applies to individuals convicted by a criminal court and who do not pay: - Fines - Procedural taxes - Compensation and mental illness to be paid to civil parties.
There must be a record of this notice. And the record must be signed or fingerprinted by the inmate. After the deadline, if the prisoner does not pay, the prosecutor has the right to order, detain and detain, but the prosecutor can also delay the decision due to humanitarian reasons, such as the prisoner's medical condition.
In the event of a dispute between fines, taxes, procedures and compensation and compensation, the compensation and compensation shall be given priority.
In conclusion, physical coercion is aimed at forcing prisoners to pay fines, taxes, procedures, as well as compensation and damages to civil parties, in particular to ensure the effective implementation of criminal court decisions. Forced to this body is also understood under the humanitarian image.
