The young CEO of "Hong Lai Huot" also revealed the reasons why the company is investing in agriculture in Cambodia. According to Mr. Di Lang Ong, seizing the opportunity to invest in agriculture in Cambodia because Cambodia is a country with a long history of agriculture in Asia and has strong support from the Royal Government to investors.
The foothills of the Oral district were originally just remote areas, but have now been transformed into cassava fields. For more than 12 years, the company has been actively involved in the business of growing and processing cassava flour for export to international markets. But before reaping the benefits of the agribusiness as it is today, the company overcame many obstacles.
Mr. Di Lang Ong described the obstacles and difficulties that the company has gone through: "The early stages of the establishment of farms in Cambodia are very difficult due to the lack of electricity, roads and water supply, and these problems "It provides a big hurdle for companies to overcome."
Currently, HLH Agriculture is focusing on growing potatoes and processing them to market, which is growing year by year. On a vast expanse of mountains in a remote area of Oral County, the company plans to grow cassava year-round in order to supply its production chain to the market.
Cassava flour produced by HLH factory is gaining more and more support from its overseas customers. Ms. Long Taiwa, manager of HLH flour factory, said that cassava can be processed into food, cosmetics and some household items.
Ms. Long Taiwa said that at present, HLH's cassava processing plant can produce about 120 tons of noodles per day, and between 1,500 and 2,000 tons of cassava per month to supply its customers outside. Country and local.
During the global epidemic of Kovid 19, many businesses went into crisis and some were shut down. But HLH continues to do well and get more cassava flour orders.
Attracting foreign investors is a policy that the Royal Government of Cambodia has always taken into account and has always set out a strategy to attract foreign investors to Cambodia. Foreign investment not only helps to generate income through the payment of taxes to support the national economy, but also contributes to providing jobs and improving the lives of Cambodians.
Say Sopheak, 34, a resident of Banteay Meanchey province, has been working in a potato farm for almost two years. The potato worker said that the work in the potato fields is regular in both the dry and wet seasons because the company has been planting potatoes in succession.
Mr. Sopheak confirmed that "I think it is enough because he has a daily income. Collectively, this is a side job in addition to their farming. So, after the farming season, he can come and work here more, so his life is better than before. That means he has another source of income in addition to farm work.
"In the long run, the company aims to increase its capacity, not just focus on processing cassava into noodles," said Di Lang Ong. But also focus on many other food production products made directly from noodles and other food ingredients that are in high demand in the market of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
To achieve this ambition and vision, the company welcomes investors who wish to set up a food processing plant to work with the company, as the company has a large plot of land ready for cooperation. Investors who wish to co-invest with the company, please contact the company at 070 828 004.
