(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ngin Chhay, Delegate of the Royal Government in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, on November 26, 2020, stated that in the intervention to restore rice cultivation after the flood in 2020 provided 6,578 tons of rice seeds will be distributed to 65,780 families for intervention in 2020.
At a press conference on "Interventions in Response to Floods in Agricultural Production" organized by the Government Spokesperson's Unit, Mr. Ngin Chhay confirmed that so far the cultivated area of rice has been restored. There are 20,585 hectares.
The senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture added that in addition to rice seeds, the Royal Government has also provided seeds, vegetables and mixed crops for intervention, as of November 25, 2020, a total of 32,376 kg to farmers. 97,966 families, each family received 4 to 6 crops.
There are 11 types of vegetables and mixed crops distributed to the people, including: Tomatoes, eggplant, eggplant, beans, soybeans, kale, kale, pumpkin, kale, kale, cucumber and cucumber.
A total of 73,266 hectares of horticultural crops were affected by the floods, totaling 10,001 hectares, of which 2,310 hectares were damaged and 84 hectares were rehabilitated.
(Phnom Penh): A senior official of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries on November 26, 2020 explained the reasons for the decline in local rice prices, which is a concern of farmers as the Ministry as well as relevant institutions. Working together on this issue to find a solution for farmers.
Mr. Ngin Chhay, the Royal Government delegate in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Agriculture, pointed out that the decline in rice prices is due to many factors. He said that supply and demand factors, market factors, weather factors and quality factors caused the price of rice to change.
At a press conference organized by the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit on "Interventions in Response to Floods in Agricultural Production", Mr. Ngin Chhay said that every year until the rice harvest season, the price There is always a decline because supply is high and procurement or processing for export is not possible.
In particular, weather factors also cause the quality of rice to decline, and when the quality of rice declines as well. At this point, he claimed that the rice that fell the most was Phkar Romduol because this type of rice suffered a lot of floods.
Despite all these challenges, Mr. Ngin Chhay claimed that the Ministry of Agriculture will try to coordinate, especially the Ministry to work with the Rice Federation and the Ministry of Commerce. He said officials have coordinated with collectors to try to help farmers buy more and not lower prices too much, even if their rice is of poor quality due to mild weather conditions.
