(Phnom Penh): As Cambodians continue to face the challenges posed by Kovid-19 and the devastation caused by the recent floods across the country, banking and financial institutions in Cambodia have been Contribute to help those affected people in this difficult time.
Since the beginning of 2020, banking and financial institutions in Cambodia have raised more than $ 500,000 to donate to the health sector, including medical supplies and groceries to support vulnerable communities.
Dr. In Channy, President of the Banking Association of Cambodia, said, “The banking and finance sector is a business with people born from the heart and driven by the desire to help people succeed and grow through formal financial services. "This desire is also expanding to help the poor in any way we can."
According to a recent survey conducted by the Association, more than half of Cambodia's banking and financial institutions have incorporated social programs into their core mission, with some committed to donating a certain percentage of their income to support the project. Social causes related to environment, education, health and financial environment.
"During the Kovid-19 outbreak, we have redoubled our efforts to help our clients, such as by restructuring more than $ 270,000 in loans to more than $ 3 billion," he added. "We are continuing this work as we become more aware of the extent of Kovid-19 disease and its impact on the economy."
Kea Boran continued, "We are also working with the members of the association to prepare for the implementation of the legal standards that are currently being drafted, in which consumer protection will also be incorporated into Cambodian law." "And this is a work that we fully support."
In addition to enforcing the law, the two associations, together with the National Bank of Cambodia, are stepping up efforts to improve financial literacy and the financial environment in Cambodia through a campaign to promote the basics of sustainable lending and lending.
The campaign will use social media, digital and social media, as well as targeted workshops for small and medium enterprises, women entrepreneurs and agribusinesses to improve the quality of lending among people who "lack banking services."
Dr. In Channy said, "Our industry is in a state of constant evolution as we are facing epidemic problems as well as opportunities arising from economic diversification, including the use of digital systems. Change the way everyone does business. ”
