(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Rural Development needs more than 91 million US dollars to restore and repair rural roads damaged by floods in the past. So far, 670 roads have been affected, amounting to 5,389.59 kilometers, of which 31.54.42 kilometers have been damaged and a total of 229 works of art have been damaged.
This is according to Mr. Chan Darong, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Rural Development, speaking at a press conference organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government on "Strategies to improve the damage affected by floods in the development sector. Rural ”on the morning of November 24, 2020.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Rural Development confirmed that of the 25 capitals and provinces, the ministry found that nine provinces were the worst affected, including Banteay Meanchey, Pursat, Battambang, Kampong Speu, Siem Reap, Pailin, Kandal, Kampong Chhnang and Oddar Meanchey. However, in those nine provinces, the Ministry selected three priority provinces that need to be repaired first: Banteay Meanchey, Pursat and Battambang.
Mr. Chan Darong said that the three priority provinces to be rehabilitated first need to spend about 16 million dollars, of which Banteay Meanchey cost 4.6 million dollars, Pursat cost 4.6 million dollars and Battambang 3. $ 3 million. And now the Ministry of Economy and Finance has dropped the $ 15 million budget for the Ministry of Rural Development to address.
Regarding rural water supply infrastructure, spokesman Chandarong said that the affected rural water supply infrastructure was water wells. A total of 4,155 wells were damaged. The total cost of repairing and rehabilitating the wells affected by the floods is more than 900,000 US dollars.
Mr. Chandarong added that based on the results of the assessment, rural roads that were cut off by the floods were immediately rehabilitated to ensure normal traffic flow and artistic structures such as bridges. And sewers that are damaged or broken are repaired and rebuilt to ensure that there is no danger or danger to the people.
He emphasized that in addition to the budget allocated to the department, the ministry also has some reserve budget for additional emergency intervention in case the department has responded but not enough due to the size of the damage. To help solve the problem, the Ministry has asked the Royal Government to increase the budget for rehabilitation after the floods recede.
(Phnom Penh): On the morning of November 24, 2020, the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit held a press conference on "Strategies to Improve Flood Damage Affected by Rural Development" with the participation of representatives. Ministry of Rural Development and coordinated by Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Government Spokesperson Unit.
The conference aims to provide the public with a clear understanding of the Royal Government's strategic policy to intervene in the rehabilitation of rural areas affected by floods in the past.
In particular, 4,155 wells were damaged. The total cost of repairing and rehabilitating the wells affected by the floods is about $ 900,000.
