The rice seed distribution ceremony was presided over by Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, and attended by Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, Mr. Thong Khon, Minister of Tourism and Team Leader. National Assistance to Kampong Thom Province and Mr. Sok Lou, Governor of Kampong Thom Province.
During the ceremony, Mr. Veng Sakhon briefed the participants on the recent floods that destroyed important physical infrastructure and engulfed tens of thousands of hectares of rice, mixed crops and livestock.
The Minister of Agriculture also said that under the strict order of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, about 6,578 tons of light rice and 150 kg were distributed to farmers for replanting.
In front of the people whose rice fields were damaged by the floods, Senior Minister Kun Kim, on behalf of Samdech Techo Prime Minister, expressed his condolences for the damage to the rice fields due to the floods. "Natural disasters are something that we all do not want to see the government, but we can not prevent or prevent natural disasters," he told the people.
On this occasion, the Senior Minister also highlighted the intervention and immediate resolution of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and all relevant officials to help people from this disaster, both when the water begins to flood and after the water recedes.
"While we can not prevent disasters, we do not leave the people unattended or rescued," he said. You see, Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit were not indifferent, when the water started to flood, Samdech ordered to pick up the people and distribute food when the water receded, Samdech Lok ordered to distribute rice seeds and intervene to "Replant."
The senior minister added, "Understanding the plight of the farmers, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has issued the necessary and urgent measures for the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, the Ministry of Water Resources and the Ministry of Rural Development to intervene immediately to help restore production. "Rice crops to the affected farmers to the best of their ability, in every way, in a timely and efficient manner, without allowing any of our relatives to die of lack of food."
Mr. Kun Kim also reminded people to make efforts to grow crops to restore livelihoods and make sure there is no shortage of rice to eat. At the same time, to ensure that people's crops do not face problems, Senior Minister Kun Kim reminded officials and relevant authorities at both national and sub-national levels to pay attention and intervene to meet the needs of farmers.
Senior Minister Kun Kim also reminded the authorities that the supply and supply of water sources is the most important task for the replanting. Therefore, it is necessary to find ways not to lack the source of irrigation water for rice, which can lead to re-cultivation of rice yields.
It should be noted that the recent floods caused by heavy rains across Kampong Thom province affected more than 18,000 hectares of rice fields and damaged more than 10,000 hectares. As of November 23, more than 80 percent of the rehabilitated area has been rehabilitated.
On the same day, after distributing rice seeds, Senior Minister Kun Kim inspected the replanting of farmers and inspected the source of canal water in Chaktolok village, Baray commune, Baray district, Kampong Thom province.
While inspecting the re-cultivation of rice, the Senior Minister facilitated the opening of sluice gates to pump water to the people who are sowing rice to ensure that the re-cultivated rice fields get enough yields.
