(Phnom Penh): On November 23, 2020, the traffic police throughout Cambodia inspected and fined a total of 623 vehicles, including 452 motorcycles and 171 cars. This was confirmed by Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun, Deputy Commissioner General and spokesman for the National Police.
According to the spokesperson of the General Commissioner, during the 207 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to November 23, 2020, the traffic police throughout the country inspected and A total of 222,424 vehicles were fined, including 164,300 motorcycles and 58,124 vehicles of all types.
The most notable offenses for motorcycles are not wearing a helmet, overtaking, not having a license plate, being drunk and using a cell phone. The offenses for cars are mostly speeding, drunken driving, drunk driving licenses, no seat belts and cell phone use.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun continued that for the past 207 days, the implementation of the new sub-decree to fine the offending vehicles has been going well. He thanked and commended all the road users for their good obedience to the traffic law so far, especially the traffic police for their dedication and hard work. Tighten traffic with the highest sense of responsibility.
It should be noted that for November 23, 2020, there were 4 traffic accidents throughout Cambodia, in which 1 person was killed and 7 people were injured (4 people were seriously injured).
