General Knowledge: In Roman times, almost two thousand years ago, a volcanic eruption killed an entire city.
Located near the foot of Mount Vesuvius, Pumpei was first built in the 7th century BC, when it was just a small town of multinational nomads as they docked on the coast. Mediterranean. As the city grew and the influence of the Roman Empire intensified, the ancient city was annexed by the Roman authorities for many generations.
Unfortunately, this peaceful city was also attacked by the eruption of Mount Vesuvius during the year 79 AD, leaving the entire city covered in haze and even the sun could not shine. Let the whole city be in darkness.
Until 1714, the city of Pumpei was rediscovered by historians, who excavated and buried the remains of a large city. Climbed to a solid rock, and many of the human and animal bodies that died during the volcanic eruption were in a state of dehydration that had almost turned to rock.
The corpses represent the most tragic death in human history, which is why historians have renamed Pumpei "the city of death."
