Spokesman for the General Commission: On November 22, another 846 vehicles were inspected and fined
(Phnom Penh): On November 22, 2020, the traffic police throughout Cambodia inspected and fined a total of 846 vehicles, including 657 motorcycles and 189 cars. This was confirmed by Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun, Deputy Commissioner General and spokesman for the National Police.
According to the spokesperson of the General Commissioner, during the 206 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to November 22, 2020, the traffic police throughout the country inspected and A total of 22,1801 vehicles were fined, including 163,384 motorcycles and 57,953 vehicles of all types.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun said that for the past 206 days, the implementation of the new sub-decree to fine vehicles for offenses has gone well. He thanked and commended all the road users for their good obedience to the traffic law so far, especially the traffic police for their dedication and hard work. Tighten traffic with the highest sense of responsibility.
It should be noted that for November 22, 2020, there were 14 traffic accidents throughout Cambodia, in which 6 people were killed and 23 were injured (13 seriously injured).
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, urged the relevant authorities at the national, sub-national and local levels to pay close attention to farming as well as crop cultivation. Back after the flood of the people. The Senior Minister reminded the authorities not to disappoint Samdech Techo Hun Sen or to make his efforts in vain.
Senior Minister Kun Kim's remarks were made during a visit to inspect the re-farming of people in Battambang, Banteay Meanchey, Pursat and Kampong Thom provinces on November 21 and 22, 2020.
This year's floods have affected many hectares of people's rice fields. The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has set out urgent measures to promote rice farming and replanting in order to help restore their livelihoods and avoid food shortages.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen Instructed Officials of Relevant Ministries, Especially the Ministry of Agriculture, Ministry of Water Resources and Ministry of Rural Development to Jointly Distribute Rice Seed, Repair Canals and Build Roads, All Measures to Support Rehabilitation Impact of flood victims
As of November 15, about 6,000 tons of non-seasonal light rice varieties and nearly 560,000 packages of mixed crops were distributed to farmers after the floods receded. In addition, Samdech Techo also released agricultural machinery and rice sowing machines to help intervene in rice farming.
Currently, the people have been actively involved in this replanting. The report shows that re-farming in Battambang and Banteay Meanchey provinces achieved more than 50%, Kampong Thom province achieved about 85% and Pursat province achieved more than 34%.
Chhean Hat, 55, a resident of Tnaot village, Kampong Svay commune, Kampong Svay district, Kampong Thom province, confirmed that he received 100 kilograms of rice seeds for replanting after his rice was submerged and damaged.
From the fields that have just been plowed and are replanting, Mr. Chhean Hat expressed his satisfaction and pleasure for the attention and timely response of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and relevant officials to enable him to farm. To support livelihoods.
Mr. Chhean Hat emphasized, "I would like to sincerely thank Samdech for not only helping the people now. "But Samdech is the one who brings countless peace and development for Cambodia. There are roads, there are schools, there is education, there is hope like today."
In front of the farmers who are growing crops again, Senior Minister Kun Kim emphasized that saving the lives of the people, raising the standard of living, rescuing the people from hardship is a great ambition and intention on the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen. This great ambition is not new, Samdech Techo, the head of the Royal Government, has been there since he left to fight for the lives of the people in 1977.
The Senior Minister said that based on this strong spirit and will, every time the people are suffering, the Samdech is always present and measures to be taken immediately to help the people.
"Samdech's wish is to see the people happy, the people happy, the people have a prosperous life. When people are suffering, it is also a difficult time for Samdech Techo. You see, throughout the year 2020, our people, Cambodia, we have suffered repeatedly due to Kovid 19 disease, difficult by floods, but Samdech Techo did not leave the people with the people to solve difficulties with the people. Senior Minister Kun Kim confirmed.
On the occasion of inspecting the re-cultivation of the people, Senior Minister Kun Kim also reminded all relevant authorities at the national, sub-national and local levels to pay close attention to the re-cultivation of the people. Citizens. He stressed the need to inspect the fields and put in place measures to address the challenges.
"Our mission is not to distribute rice, distribute seeds and that's it," he said. We must make sure that the replanting of the people benefits. Samdech Techo's intention is to see the re-cultivation of the people who have benefited, can solve food problems, can solve difficulties in life. We must not let Samdech Techo's intentions become in vain. ”
The senior minister told authorities that water was the main problem for people to replant their fields. Therefore, all authorities must come up with intervention measures to ensure that people have enough water to irrigate rice. Kovid-19 Daily Report: "The number of infected people on earth has exceeded 58.6 million, more than 1.38 million have died, more than 40 million have been cured, 197 countries have more than 100 cases »
Legal Awareness: Joint ventures
(Phnom Penh): In daily life, each person always communicates and helps each other, especially between neighbors living next to each other. After all, if a person lives without rules, rules and practices that lead to violations of the rights of others, it will inevitably lead to disputes between the villagers and the same fence.
For this reason, the Civil Code of the Kingdom of Cambodia provides for "joint ventures", which stipulate the rights and obligations of neighbors with fences or borders to prevent conflicts. To better understand what co-ownership means? And what are these rights and obligations?
Fresh News Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation would like to raise the topic of "Joint venture" to explain and share to all citizens and students as follows:
The owners of the joint ventures have a joint obligation to bear the cost of the construction of the walls, to pay for maintenance and other expenses (Article 224 of the Civil Code). In the event that any owner refuses to pay the above costs, the owner shall relinquish the rights over his wall to the person in charge of paying the costs alone.
If any evasive owner refuses to pay for the repair or reconstruction of the wall, the evasive owner shall delegate the entire wall to another sole proprietor. On the other hand, the Civil Code also stipulates ways to turn non-co-ownership walls into co-ownership walls.
In general, those who have walls, fences or other walls next to each other or share should be used responsibly to help maintain and respect each other's rights to avoid conflicts between you. Neighbors.
At the same time, citizens should also be aware of the provisions of the above joint ventures to know the extent of their rights and obligations, as well as relatives living nearby.
