The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment praised the Siem Reap Provincial Administration, together with the Department of Environment and partner organizations for working together to clean up the environment in this tourist province, and in particular the initiatives and mobilization of people involved in the collection of plastic waste. From the Tonle Sap and preventing plastic from flowing into the Tonle Sap by intercepting water sources, including rivers and streams.
Spokesman Jin Malin: Criminal incitement is not a violation of freedom of expression
(Phnom Penh): Secretary of State for the Ministry of Justice, Chin Malin, on November 22, 2020, stated that incitement under the Penal Code is not a violation of freedom of expression.
He was speaking after Radio Free Asia, which serves the rebels, reported that civil society officials LICADHO and the US-based Clooney Foundation had asked the Cambodian government to remove incitement from the penal code. A senior official at the Ministry of Justice said that those who demanded that the Royal Government of Cambodia be a pro-opposition group that committed too many crimes and could not escape the law.
"Incitement in the Penal Code is not a violation of freedom of expression, because freedom of expression is not a freedom to spread false information, exaggerate and violate your rights," said Jean Malin. Others will affect public order and social security. The same provocation is enshrined in the criminal laws of other developed countries and democracies.
According to Jean Malin, the provision of multiple criminal provisions in the Penal Code is to protect the rights and freedoms of individuals and public order in response to developments in all areas and social aspects. The abolition of incitement will lead to anarchy and encourage arbitrary and irresponsible expression, affecting social security, public order and individual freedoms.
He suggested that as responsible citizens and if they do not want to face the law again, they should stop acting illegally under the guise of freedom of expression, rather than criticizing and demanding. To amend or repeal the provisions of this law.
