For Cambodia, during the difficult period caused by Kovid 19, it is seen that the agricultural sector continues to be active and has improved remarkably well.
Since 2009, the company has invested about $ 60 million in the ELC, clearing and growing cassava for export. To date, the company has cleared and cultivated about 90 percent of the land and is exporting about 500 tons to more than 1,000 tons of cassava flour to European and Indian markets per month.
During the 1940 Kovid outbreak in 2020, the group did not suffer from any problems or interruptions. The company continues to cultivate, harvest and process into flour, export to foreign markets, and orders increase.
Currently, between 300 and 400 workers have been working at the cassava plantation site and are being paid according to their skills and abilities. Currently, workers at the agricultural company earn between $ 250 and $ 700 a month.
Vet Piseth, a contractor for potato tuber collection at the company, said in an interview with Fresh News that during the 19-month Kovid outbreak 10 months ago, the Singaporean potato company was operating normally. Cultivation, harvesting and processing of flour.
Mr. Vet Piseth also claimed that during this period, he also received many workers who lost their jobs in the construction sector, garment factories and work in the tourism sector, some returned to work to earn a living wage.
According to Piseth, before the 19-year epidemic, finding potatoes was difficult to find because most people did not want to work in the potato fields and opted for paid construction and garment jobs. Higher and easier than working in a potato field. But after the crisis and losing their jobs, they returned to work in the potato fields.
