(Phnom Penh): Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesman of the Ministry of Environment, on November 21, 2020, said that in the coming days, Cambodia will receive an Asian elephant as a gift from the Government of Pakistan to take. Reproduced with Cambodian elephants and released in the Kulen Prom Tep Wildlife Sanctuary in Oddar Meanchey province.
Regarding the sending of Asian elephants from Pakistan to Cambodia, Mr. Neth Pheaktra said, "Now the procedure for sending this elephant to Cambodia is as planned and coordinated between CWS and an organization in Pakistan are planning to fly.
Mr. Pheaktra added that there are currently between 400 and 600 Asian elephants in Cambodia, most of which live in the Cardamom Mountains, the northern plateau of the Tonle Sap and the eastern highlands of Mondulkiri province. The area is rich in biodiversity, ecosystems and has slopes that are conducive to the survival of elephants and other wildlife. The number of domestic elephants is estimated at 71.
According to expert research, in the last 60 to 70 years, the number of Asian elephants in the world has decreased by about 50 percent, while their habitat has lost nearly 90 percent. Of the total number of Asian elephants, 39,463 to 47,427 are currently living in the wild. About 75% of them live in India and Sri Lanka.
At the same time, in addition to being seriously threatened throughout its sanctuary. Experts also warn that due to the slow reproductive period of Asian elephants (five years giving birth to a calf), if the death rate of baby elephants is high due to trapping injuries, the number of wild elephants in Cambodia could increase. High risk of extinction. That is why elephants are listed as endangered on the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List.
