Members of the Coalition for Democracy reacted strongly after the Constitutional Council on November 17, 2020 decided to dismiss its case.
In a statement issued on November 19, 2020, the Constitutional Council stated that the hearing of the Constitutional Council on November 17, 2020, examining and deciding on this case, was carried out in accordance with the spirit of the Constitution. Election of the National Assembly and the Law on the Amendment of Article 26, Article 27 (new), Article 28, Article 31 and Article 32 of the Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Constitutional Council.
Mr. Mak Taing You complained to the Constitutional Council on November 10, 2020 at 14:15, dissatisfied with the NEC's Resolution No. 001 dated November 6, 2020. When receiving a complaint, the Constitutional Council examines two important conditions: first, the competence of the Constitutional Council in resolving the complaint, and second, the qualifications (qualifications) of the complainant.
Pursuant to the new Article 28 of the Law on the Amendment of Article 26, Article 27 (new), Article 28, Article 31 and Article 32 of the Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Constitutional Council, The Constitutional Council decided to dismiss the case without going through a rationale.
Therefore, the Constitutional Council could not bring the contents of the complaint of the Coalition for Democracy to discuss, nor did it examine or discuss the decision of the NEC, which resulted in no summoning of any relevant parties. Of the plaintiff to question or examine the evidence. In the spirit of the new Article 142 of the Constitution, the decision of the Constitutional Council is a decision closing the way for protests.
