1. A Brief History of the Establishment of the Arab League The Arab League was established on March 22, 1945 with the main purpose of becoming an Arab regional institution with a focus on economic development, peaceful settlement of disputes, and political coordination between the two countries. Each other. The Arab League is an organization that covers an area of 13,333,296 square kilometers and has a population of over 400 million.
Each country has a single vote in the decisions of the Arab League, which has binding force only on the states that voted in favor. The first purpose of the Arab League in 1945 was to strengthen and coordinate political, economic, cultural and social programs for its member states and to act as a mediator among its member states in dispute as a third party.
2. Political and Administrative System of the Arab League The Constitution of the Arab League sets out a principle called the "Arab Homeland", which requires all Arab states to respect each other's sovereignty. In addition, the rules of procedure of the Arab Council and other commissions were approved in May 1951, and the provisions of the Secretariat were approved in May 1953.
Since then, governance within the Arab League has been governed by the dualist system of national institutions and the sovereignty of each member state. At its summit in 1964 in Carroll, the Arab League initiated the formation of an organization representing the Palestinian people, first on May 29, 1964, east of Jerusalem.
It was then that the Palestinian People's Liberation Organization was formed on June 2, 1964. Palestine is currently a full member of the Arab League, represented by the Palestinian People's Liberation Organization.
3: Problems of the Arab League The first major action of the Arab League was the collective intervention as the majority of the Arab nation on the emergence of the state of Israel in 1948, although the intervention did not involve Sudan. Recognize the emergence of Israel as proposed by the United Nations General Assembly.
On 22 February 2011, after the start of the civil war in Libya and the use of military force to suppress the civilian population, the Secretary-General of the Arab League claimed that Libya's membership in the Arab League had been suspended and that the organization had decided to suspend you. Also represented by the Libyan delegation in the Arab League. The suspension of Libya's membership is the second in the history of the Arab League.
Another problem is that in December 2017, the United States under the administration of President Donald Trump recognized Jerusalem as the capital of Israel. The Arab League foreign ministers have issued a statement urging the United States to rescind its decision to recognize Jerusalem as the capital of Israel after a meeting in Cairo, Egypt.
