The hearing was held at the White House of Chamkar Mon State Palace under the chairmanship of Mr. Im Chhun Lim, President of the Constitutional Council, and was attended by eight members.
After reviewing and discussing the complaint, the Constitutional Council decided to drop the complaint and block the appeal.
Prohm Vichet Akhara, spokesman for the Constitutional Council, told Fresh News that the dismissal of the complaint was due to the ineligibility of the complainant to file a complaint with the Constitutional Council in accordance with the law. This case is considered illegal because the complainant is not directly influenced by the NEC decision.
According to the spokesperson, the decision of the Constitutional Council is based on Article 164 of the Election Law and is based on the new Article 26.6 and Article 28 of the new Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Constitutional Council.
Article 164 of the Election Law states that persons directly affected by the decision of the NEC may file a written complaint to the Constitutional Council within 72 hours of the date of receipt of the decision. .
Separately, the new Article 28 of the Law on the Amendment of Article 26, Article 27 (new), Article 28, Article 31 and Article 32 of the Law on the Organization and Functioning of the Constitutional Council stipulates that the complaint is illegal and the shortcomings cannot be corrected. The Constitutional Council may decide to dismiss the case without going through a rationale. .
Mr. Prohm Vichet Akhra stated, "The person who filed the complaint with the Constitutional Council is not qualified to file a complaint because he is not directly affected by the decision of the NEC. Which is illegal. That is why this morning's hearing saw all the legal points, so the Constitutional Council decided to drop the lawsuit.
Please be reminded that on October 26, 2020, Mr. Mak Taing You, the representative on behalf of Neou Sam Ath, who represented the first plaintiff, Yon Yoeum, an agent of the Coalition Party for Democracy, complained to the NEC to fine the group. Voting names and cancellations of some residents in Buor commune, Phnom Preuk district, Battambang province, after they claimed that the registration was in violation of the voter registration procedures.
After receiving the complaint, the NEC examined and researched all these issues and reached a decision on November 6, 2020. The Trial Chamber of the NEC has decided to dismiss the lawsuit in violation of the law.
The Trial Chamber added that the voter registration team of Buor commune did not commit fraudulently registering voters who did not meet the conditions to vote. The voter registration team of Buor commune did not forge any identification documents for anyone to use for voter registration.
The Trial Chamber also found that the Chairman of the Voter Registration Team of Buor Commune improperly carried out the voter registration procedure in accordance with the principles set out in Section 6.4.24 of the Regulations and Procedures for the Examination of the List. And the annual voter registration, which the NEC should remind the leader of the voter registration team to know about the mistake and avoid repeating it later.
The decision also states that any objection to the right to be on the voter list must be made through an objection when the initial voter list is posted under the conditions set out in Article 61 of the Election Law. Elect MPs.
The decision of the NEC Chamber also stated that Mr. Taing Yu's complaint had no proper legal basis, which could have imposed a penalty on the NEC in accordance with the provisions of the NEC. Article 141 of the Law on the Election of the National Assembly.
