The reaction of the Cambodian representative to the United Nations came after Mary Lawlor on 16 November 2020 issued a statement on the situation of human rights defenders in Cambodia. In the statement, she expressed concern over the legal action against human rights defenders and highlighted the arrests of several people, including Rong Chhun.
In a statement in response to this, Mr. An Sok Khoeun, Ambassador and Permanent Representative of Cambodia to the United Nations, stated that the assessment of a country can only be trusted based on the context of law and facts. On the spot with verified sources of information and a shared vision.
Mr. Sokhoeun said she had not verified the information obtained from government sources. A closer look reveals that her statement was biased, one-sided and damaging.
The Cambodian representative added that Professor Lawlor had never said that the exercise of freedom of expression, association and peaceful assembly should be linked to special cases, responsibilities and restrictions imposed by Laws as stated in Article 29 (2) of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, as well as Article 19 (3), Article 21 and Article 22 (2) of the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights.
"It is the duty of everyone, including the so-called , to exercise their rights responsibly within the bounds of the law." In the end, it seems that the professor has not been fully informed about the exercise of individual rights and freedoms, which must be in accordance with the law as stated in Article 31 of the Constitution of Cambodia. This is further emphasized in the statement of Mr. An Sok Khoeun.
"A peaceful gathering that cooperates with the authorities and adheres to security and safety measures is an exercise of constitutional and legal freedoms," he added. "On the contrary, gathering to put pressure on the judiciary is an offense under Article 522 of the Cambodian Penal Code."
In this statement, Mr. Sokhoeun reminded the Special Rapporteur that joining any political party or non-governmental organization is not giving an individual the right to violate the law with impunity. An offense under the law is an offense and can not be used as an argument for any other cause.
