The Ministry of Health has issued instructions to people to be careful and protect their health during cold weather (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Health this morning, November 16, 2020, issued instructions calling on people to be careful. Be careful to protect your health during cold weather.
1. Among the most common seasonal diseases during the cold season between December and January of the year are the most common respiratory diseases that are not suitable for cold weather or allergies and respiratory diseases. Viruses and Streptococcus viruses that cause symptoms such as the common cold, runny nose, sore throat, cough and gastroenteritis caused by viruses.
Here are some simple steps you can take to take care of yourself and your family, especially the elderly, babies and children:
Wear a thick coat, a scarf, a hat and winter gloves to prevent the cold from touching the body openly to maintain normal body temperature.
Seek medical attention at a health facility if you have symptoms of shortness of breath, fever, cough, sore throat and muscle aches or other health problems not listed in this guide.
Remember to travel only when you are healthy, and if you think you have a cold or are not feeling well, postpone your travel plans until you are at least 24 minutes comfortable. Hours after the onset of discomfort, do not take over-the-counter antibiotics.
When communicating with sick people, they must know how to protect themselves from infection without being too close and take measures as mentioned above.
2. Infants, children and the elderly are at high risk for cold-related illnesses. Babies lose body heat faster than adults. In addition, babies and young children can not maintain adequate body temperature without the proper help of their caregivers.
Therefore, the following points may be helpful in understanding for their protection:
Babies less than one year old should not sleep in a cold room. Properly providing warm clothing and blankets for babies to try to keep their body warm as well as in the baby's room.
Older people often have low body temperature due to slow metabolism and decreased or decreased physical activity, which can cause the body temperature to drop below normal. Therefore, the elderly are also at risk, which can be life-threatening due to the body's response to cold, especially those with diabetes and heart disease.
Children and the elderly should wear layers of clothing when the weather is cold to keep them warm. Avoid tight clothing because it can interfere with blood circulation in the body and cause loss of body heat.
Children and the elderly should wear hats and socks when going out in cold weather to keep body heat from being lost through the arms, head and legs.
Drink water frequently to avoid dehydration as the body struggles to cope with the cold.
