Legal Awareness: Judicial Review
(Phnom Penh): When a person commits a criminal offense, the prosecutor will charge and send that person to the investigating judge for investigation, charge or acquittal, and if the investigating judge finds that the act Committing a felony or misdemeanor causing serious harm to society, the investigating judge may detain the individual to end or prevent the recurrence of the offense and not to affect the investigative proceedings.
However, if the act has caused minor harm to the society and there is a legitimate reason that does not affect the investigative procedure, the investigating judge may decide to place the accused under judicial supervision. At all times, under investigation.
To better understand this week, Fresh News, in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of "judicial review" to explain to the following students:
As part of the remedial measures to facilitate the conduct of the investigation, the investigating judge may place the accused under judicial supervision (out of jail) or decide on pretrial detention (in custody).
For placement under judicial supervision, the content of the obligation may be amended or canceled and new obligations may be added in accordance with three (3) conditions: (1) the modification of the judicial review by the investigating judge Ask and (2) the petition to amend the judicial review by the prosecutor and (3) through the petition to modify the judicial review by the accused.
In the event that the accused deliberately evades the obligation of judicial review, the investigating judge may decide to temporarily detain the accused, regardless of the length of the prison sentence and whether The accused was remanded in custody for a maximum period of time.
In summary, a judicial review is a special procedure for the court (investigating judge) to administer a defendant on bail, giving the investigating judge the power to impose certain obligations or prohibitions on Defendant.
