(Tbong Khmum): A baby male dolphin was found dead along the Mekong River in Roka Khnor commune, Krouch Chhmar district, Tbong Khmum province, more than 50 kilometers from where they live in Kratie province. This was confirmed by Kroch Chhmar District Authority and WWF to Fresh News on November 15.
The dead dolphin was about seven days old, weighed 12 kilograms and was 103 centimeters long. Its body was found on November 14, 2020.
According to Kroch Chhmar district authorities, it is not yet known why the baby dolphin died. Although the cause of the dolphin's death appears to be ambiguous, WWF experts have cast doubt on the use of illegal fishing gear, which threatens their survival.
According to the latest WWF census, there are currently only 89 dolphins left in the Mekong River in Cambodia.
Experts point to a number of issues that threaten the survival of dolphins in Cambodia, including: Construction of dams on the Mekong River * Climate change.
Please be informed that the dolphin is a rare species in the world that many organizations have been involved in conservation, WWF has worked closely with the Royal Government of Cambodia to participate in the conservation of dolphins, but due to a number of factors affected. To their life and keep killing them.
