Kun Nhem Attends Asia-Pacific Customs Director General Conference and Regional Liaison Meeting via Video
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kun Nhim, Director General of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia, on November 12, 2020, led a senior official of the General Department of Customs and Excise of Cambodia to attend the Asia-Pacific Customs Director General Conference of the World Customs Organization. The 21st and the 30th Meeting of Regional Communications Officials via video.
Speaking at the opening of the conference, Heru Pambudi thanked the Secretary-General of the World Customs Organization, the Director-General of Customs and the Representative of the Regional Director-General for Customs and discussed the development of the organization's Asia-Pacific Strategic Plan. World Customs for 2020-2022 in accordance with the strategic plan of the World Customs Organization.
On that occasion, Mr. Kunio Mikuriya participated as a speaker and raised the current topic of the World Customs Organization's policies, initiatives and strategic plans in response to the common challenges of the Kovid epidemic 19, he said. Prepare a number of guidelines for the customs administration to implement during the outbreak.
The meeting heard the report of the World Customs Organization for Asia-Pacific Strategic Plan for 2020-2022, which focused on three key areas: security and trade facilitation, compliance and law enforcement and capacity building.
On that occasion, the meeting supported the nomination of Asia-Pacific countries to the Policy Committee and the Audit Committee of the World Customs Organization and approved the training centers of the Customs Administration of Indonesia and Iran as regional training centers, customs laboratories. Of the Indian and Indonesian Customs Administration as regional customs laboratories, Korea K-9 Dog Training Center as regional K-9 dog training center.
The 22nd Asia-Pacific Customs Director-General Conference of the World Customs Organization is scheduled to take place from April 6 to 8, 2021.
