General knowledge: Which countries produce and use the most robots?
According to the International Federation of Robotics (IFR) statistics, Japan is the largest producer of robots in the world, followed by Germany.
Notably, Japan is a major player in the robotics industry and the largest exporter of robots, accounting for 56% of the world's total robot supply.
The country that uses the most robots is also Japan. Out of 10,000 workers, there are already 300 robots. They are most commonly used in the automotive industry.
Apart from Japan, China and South Korea also use the most robots. This is due to the rapid economic growth, and the industrial sector is also very busy, which requires the most robotic manpower to work.
According to the International Robotics Federation, in Europe, the rate of use of robots is 106 per 10,000 workers, while in the United States and Asia, the rate of use of robots is 75-91. Among the same 10,000 workers. Journalist arrested for illegally transporting rosewood from Siem Pang district to Kratie province
(Stung Treng): Military police in Stung Treng and Kratie provinces have arrested a journalist in connection with a case of smuggling illegal timber with a Camry truck traveling from Siem Pang district, Stung Treng province to Kratie province.
The military police officer who cracked down on the operation confirmed to Fresh News Agency on November 13, 2020 that the suspect and the exhibits were detained at more than 2 pm on November 12, 2020 on National Road 7 in front of O'Preah pagoda in O'Preah commune, Sambo district, Kratie province.
"Before the crackdown, the police chased the car while transporting rosewood from Siem Pang district," the report said. Upon arrival at the military security office in Dong Kralor, the military chased the car until it reached Kratie province and caught both the car and the driver in front of Wat O Preah of the above base.
According to the report, the truck carrying rosewood that the military police cracked down on at that time was a moss-colored Camry Shark with license plate number Kampong Speu 2A-2025. The driver of the car, Tan Sopheak, is the assistant editor-in-chief of a local news agency stationed in Kratie province.
After the arrest, the suspect and the truck were sent to Stung Treng Provincial Gendarmerie Command. The exact amount of rosewood in the car is not yet known, as the evidence of the crime was handed over to the Stung Treng Provincial Forestry Officer to build a case for legal proceedings. Notice! Kampong Cham provincial authorities set a 30-day deadline to transfer land ownership
(Kampong Cham): In the 2nd Dissemination and Consultation Forum of the Provincial Council of the 3rd Mandate 2020 in the area of Boeung Snay Pagoda in Sambour Meas Commune, Kampong Cham City, Kampong Cham Provincial Administration decided to reduce the time for land transfer service. People do not exceed 30 days.
The outreach and consultation forum was chaired by Mr. Khlot Phorn, Chairman of Kampong Cham Provincial Council and Mr. Un Chanda, Provincial Governor, with the participation of 161 relevant officials and residents of Kampong Cham City.
In addition, in this consultation forum, the provincial authorities received 13 requests from the people, of which 7 were resolved and 6 were left to be resolved later.
However, the above 13 points are an oral request from a citizen related to the delay in the land transfer service, which in the past, this service was delayed up to 6 months to get results.
However, after receiving the request, Mr. Un Chanda immediately responded that "the issue of land transfer, the provincial administration has already set a common principle to streamline the procedure to ensure the transfer of land ownership from one person to another. The fastest is not more than 30 days.
At the same time, Mr. Un Chanda emphasized that the parties to the documents must follow the instructions of the Cadastral Officer by preparing the documents properly from the beginning, because to avoid insufficient documents that cause the documents to be returned. Returning leads to delays.
