China tells US to suspend ties with Taiwan
BEIJING: China on Wednesday urged the United States to suspend ties with Taiwan after Washington and Taipei announced they would hold economic talks in November, and the Taiwanese government called on the United States to step down. The talks are described as a "significant milestone" in relations between the two countries. This is according to a release by Reuters on Wednesday, November 11, 2020.
China considers its democratic-ruled Taiwan to have no right to establish formal ties with other countries. But recently, China has seen concerns that Taiwan has received US support, such as new arms sales and visits to Taipei by senior US officials.
Speaking at a daily news conference in Beijing, Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin said: "China has strongly opposed any official exchanges between Washington and Taipei. "To the United States to suspend any official exchanges involving Taiwan and to stop the escalation of this important relationship."
The Taipei government has also said that Taiwan will send a small delegation to Washington, led by Deputy Economy Minister Chen Chern-chy, to attend the US Economic Prosperity Partnership Meeting. Taiwan, which is scheduled to open on November 20.
