Spokesman for the General Commission: On November 11, another 906 vehicles were inspected and fined
(Phnom Penh): On November 11, 2020, the traffic police throughout Cambodia inspected and fined a total of 906 vehicles, including 647 motorcycles and 259 cars. This was confirmed by Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun, Deputy Commissioner General and spokesman for the National Police.
According to the spokesperson of the General Commissioner, during the 195 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to November 11, 2020, the traffic police throughout the country inspected and A total of 211,701 vehicles were fined, including 156,491 motorcycles and 55,210 vehicles of all types.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun said that for the past 195 days, the implementation of the new sub-decree to fine the offending vehicles has been going well. He thanked and commended all the road users for their good obedience to the traffic law so far, especially the traffic police for their dedication and hard work. Tighten traffic with the highest sense of responsibility.
It should be noted that for November 11, 2020, there were 6 traffic accidents throughout Cambodia, in which 7 people were killed and 4 people were injured (2 people were seriously injured).
National Authority for Combating Drugs Receives 50,000 More Drug Promotion Materials for Distribution
(Phnom Penh): On 11 November 2020, LOLC Microfinance Institution handed over 50,000 drug leaflets to the General Secretariat of the National Authority for Combating Drugs for dissemination. Drug problem to the people through the front line officials at the commune level and the member ministries of the National Authority for Combating Drugs.
Mr. Sok Voeun, CEO of LOLC Microfinance Institution, said, "The fight against drugs is our collective work and can not be considered the exclusive work of any one person or group. "Starting with all of us, the elimination of drugs is a major common problem that we must all work together to avoid danger and bring disaster to ourselves, our families and our society."
He urged all stakeholders and all citizens to work together to prevent the spread of the drug problem that is destroying our community. He added that the LOLC will continue to fully support the National Anti-Drug Authority to join the fight against drugs.
Lt. Gen. Lim Tong Huot considers LOLC as an active partner that has always supported the General Secretariat of the National Authority for Combating Drugs on Outreach, Education, Combat Drugs and Assisted Social Work. These include providing clean water wells, hand washing facilities in schools, combating the epidemic of Kovid-19 traffic, and assisting in the rescue of flood victims. Etc.
He added that the implementation of a multi-sectoral strategy to combat drugs is needed from all sectors, especially the private sector, as the LOLC has been doing both now and in the future to ensure safety and order. To all people, no drugs, no violence and no crime.
Please note that this is the seventh time that LOLC has submitted to the General Secretariat of the National Authority for Combating Drugs a leaflet on drug issues, which reflects the LOLC's commitment to Continue to participate in the fight against drugs in Cambodia.
