Ministry of Health Guidelines on Implementing the Social Safety Gap and the Personal Safety Gap to Prevent and Prevent Virus-19 Transmission in the Community
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Health on November 10, 2020, issued guidelines on the implementation of the social safety gap and personal safety gap to prevent and prevent the spread of HIV-19 in communities in Cambodia. This is according to the instructions that Fresh News received a little earlier.
According to the guidelines of the Ministry of Health, Kovid-19 is transmitted by contact with droplets that fall from one person to another when an infected person with Kovid-19 virus laughs, sings, coughs or sneezes. Original.
First: Maintaining the personal safety gap by:
Second: Maintaining the social security gap by:
* Avoid or, if possible, reduce the number of social gatherings to a maximum, such as: weddings, festivals, such as gatherings in national festivals, religious ceremonies, festivals, or ceremonies. Festivals, concerts, outings, meals with friends outside the home, gatherings, birthday parties or large-scale housewarming parties, excursions and more.
Avoid group workouts at clubs or group workouts in crowded places.
* People at high risk of contracting the disease, such as the elderly, the chronically ill, pregnant women and young children, should not attend meetings or, if necessary, take precautions. Good and provide alternative means, for example, use a mobile phone, use voicemail, use video communication for their participation.
* Cancel or postpone gatherings or meetings and events that cause crowds
* Do not go out to crowded places unless absolutely necessary.
In addition to the implementation of the individual safety gaps and the social safety gaps recommended above, the public should always maintain good hygiene by washing their hands with clean water with soap or alcohol, washing their hands frequently, and wearing a mask. Regularly (if necessary) or use a towel or scarf or paper towel to cover mouth and nose when coughing or sneezing.
