Australia welcomes Biden to bring US back to Paris climate deal
The Paris Climate Agreement, which entered into force on November 4, 2016, aims to keep global average temperatures below 2 degrees Celsius by reducing greenhouse gas emissions.
Last week, Donald Trump's administration formally withdrew from the Paris Agreement. But Biden, who declared victory in the November 3 US presidential election, has vowed to bring the United States back into the deal and lead the country toward carbon neutrality by 2050.
(Phnom Penh): Peace Operation is a global principle and has been playing an important role through the participation of all national and international circles, joint operations to help countries torn apart by the fires of war, and to join countries in Rehabilitate peace, stability and development in line with UN principles to build a path to a world of peace and progress.
Over the past 14 years, the Cambodian Blue Hat Army has achieved great prestige for the nation in peacekeeping missions under the UN umbrella in countries devastated by war and chronic internal conflict.
In this context, the Kingdom of Cambodia, which had gone through nearly three decades of war and only achieved full peace throughout the country in 1998 under the win-win policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has integrated from within Asia by taking steps to expand the scope of Its rich experience contributes to peacekeeping missions in nine countries, including 10 missions in Africa, the Middle East and Europe from 2006 to the present and beyond.
Following the signing of the Paris Peace Accords on October 23, 1991, the United Nations Security Council sent a peacekeeping force to Cambodia in February 1992, known as UNTAC. He returned to Cambodia in September 1993 after 18 months of operations in Cambodia.
Due to the victorious policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, after military integration in 1998, Cambodia achieved complete peace with a single national unity. Based on this peace factor, the UN Secretary-General requested that Cambodia send troops to participate in peacekeeping missions with the National Committee in coordination with the United Nations and relevant ministries and institutions.
From 2006 to 2020, Cambodia has sent 7,123 peacekeepers, including 419 women, to UN missions in nine countries: Sudan, South Sudan, Chad. , Lebanon, Syria, Mali, Central Africa, Cyprus and Yemen on Demining, Explosive Ordnance Disposal, Military Level 2 Hospital, Horizontal-Vertical Engineering, Airport Engineering, Army Staff, Staff and Military Observer.
At a time when the world is suffering from the epidemic of Kovid-19, the National Peacekeeping Center has taken drastic measures to work with the government to prevent and control this deadly virus.
The 18-storey building, a precious gift from the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, binds the National Peacekeeping Center.
General Sem Sovanny and all his subordinates are deeply grateful to Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, for always providing support to the National Center for Qualifying the Cambodian Blue Hat Forces to participate. Carrying out joint missions with countries donating forces under the United Nations umbrella to build the prestige of the motherland should be proud of.
General Sem Sovanny confirmed that for the past 15 years since the establishment of the Cambodian Blue Hat Forces in 2005-2020, this 18-storey building is the property of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia. Facilitate the Cambodian Blue Hat Institution to operate with all relevant institutions, both domestically and with countries around the world, to participate in UN missions.
The 18-storey building above shows the excellence of the Cambodian Blue Hat Forces in 2023, which Cambodia will host a tripartite peacekeeping training course (United Nations, Japan, Cambodia).
Spokesman for the General Commission: On November 9, another 644 vehicles were inspected and fined
(Phnom Penh): On November 9, 2020, the traffic police throughout Cambodia inspected and fined a total of 644 vehicles, including 462 motorcycles and 182 cars. This was confirmed by Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun, Deputy Commissioner General and spokesman for the National Police.
According to the spokesperson of the General Commissioner, during the 193 days of tightening the road traffic law by increasing the fine according to Sub-Decree No. 39, from May 1 to November 9, 2020, the traffic police throughout the country inspected and A total of 210,030 vehicles were fined, including 155,292 motorcycles and 54,738 vehicles of all types.
Notable offenses for motorbikes are mostly without helmets, offenses, without license plates, drunkenness and using mobile phones. The offenses for cars are mostly speeding, drunken driving, no driver's license and no seat belt.
Lt. Gen. Chhay Kim Khoeun continued that for the past 193 days, the implementation of the new sub-decree to fine the offending vehicles has been going well. He thanked and commended all the road users for their good obedience to the traffic law so far, especially the traffic police for their dedication and hard work. Tighten traffic with the highest sense of responsibility.
Please note that for November 9, 2020, traffic accidents throughout the country also occurred 10 times, in which 4 people died, 12 people were injured (08 people were seriously injured).
