Around this issue, health experts have expressed concern that if the air quality continues to be high, it could actually be dangerous for patients with chronic conditions. "We are continuing to record more cases of COVID-19 infection after air quality began to decline and from now on the situation will get worse," said a chest surgeon in New Delhi. .
India has the second highest number of COVID-19 infections in the world after the United States, with more than 8.4 million cases, with at least 125,000 deaths. The number of daily cases across India seems to have dropped slightly since mid-September, but recently cases in New Delhi have picked up again.
As of Thursday, New Delhi had recorded about 7,178 new infections. An increase in recent cases has been reported at a time when India is celebrating major religious festivals, a time when crowds are gathering.
However, the link between pollution and the severity of COVID-19 remains theoretical. Toxic air temperature should also be considered as an important element of the coronavirus outbreak.
Every winter, the air pollution level in the capital rises to dangerous levels with yellow-black fog covering the entire city, which can last for months. In addition, the burning of crop residues on farms in nearby states also released large amounts of smoke that drifted to New Delhi.
The New Delhi government has been stepping up efforts in the fight against air pollution by 2020, setting up bases to monitor areas with the highest levels of pollution, using demolition measures. Dust in the air, including reducing the burning of agricultural waste.
