The wait for the election results has sparked tensions across the country as Tram continues to make allegations without evidence that the Democrats rigged the vote. But delays or delays in the counting of ballots are due to the fact that each state is not under the common electoral system.
California, the most populous state, declared victory for Biden shortly after the close of the election on Tuesday, but the announcement was based on news estimates. Waiting for the official results to come out means that the actual vote count will take longer to determine the official results in the state, but it will not be different from the previous results.
Similarly, the competitive state of Nevada will continue to count the ballots stamped on Election Day until November 10. Other reasons for the delay are ballots issued to voters if they are confused about their registration and need to be re-verified.
Coincidentally, with concerns about the spread of the COVID-19 pandemic, states that had previously received a limited number of absentee ballots (this time in absentia) received a large number of such absentee ballots. Excess because people do not want to risk going to the polls directly. An estimated 65.2 million Americans out of 160 million went to the polls by mail.
APSARA Authority and World Temple Fund discuss technical techniques for restoring Ta Som temple
