(Phnom Penh): A criminal case involving two female suspects who imported, stored, sold and distributed counterfeit Pedro products without the permission of the original company has been sent to court by the Secretariat of the Anti-Counterfeit Products Committee after being summoned by a judicial police officer. Twice refused to enter. This is according to the competent official told Fresh News on the morning of November 6, 2020.
After a thorough search, the joint police found counterfeit Pedro products, including:
Authorities confirmed that the Deputy Prosecutor has decided to temporarily confiscate all exhibits to be stored at the Anti-Counterfeit Products Committee (CDC) and set the location owner Seng Sokunthea to explain to the Combat Committee. Anti-counterfeit products at 10:00 am on October 9, 2020 to continue the procedure.
Police said that based on the results of research and quotes from Seng Sokunthea, he clarified that the pedro products that the police found were imported from Vietnam and China through a shipping company and then paid for them. The invoice is written in Chinese and it started selling Pedro products three months ago: wallets, headbands and belts.
According to the research and inspection of the police, Seng Sokunthea did not have any documents related to his goods (fake Pedro brand products) to show to the police, and when the police made two summons to answer more Seng Sokunthea was also not summoned.
The authorities consider this activity to be contrary to the Law on Trademarks and the act of dishonest competition, Article 11, Article 64.
