Minister Keut Rith: Together to prevent and fight against illegal drugs, counterfeit products is for the common benefit of society
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice, this morning, November 5, 2020, presided over a ceremony to destroy more than 540 tons of illegal drugs and counterfeit products at the landfill in Dangkor district.
The ceremony was also attended by General Miech Sophanna, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Chairman of the Anti-Counterfeiting Committee, Secretary of State and Undersecretary of State of the Ministry of Justice, as well as civil servants, the Armed Forces, local authorities. , And many national and international guests as well.
Speaking at the ceremony, Mr. Keut Rith emphasized that the issue of counterfeit medicines and counterfeit products is a public health issue and an important priority that needs to be taken seriously and timely.
He stressed that "the cooperation of law enforcement officials and the people in preventing and combating illegal drugs and counterfeit products is for the common benefit of protecting the health and lives of the people and avoiding risks." "Caused by illegal drugs and counterfeit products for society."
Minister Keut Rith also praised and commended the efforts of the Anti-Counterfeiting Committee, local authorities, judicial police officers, as well as the judiciary and other relevant law enforcement agencies, as well as their cooperation. Excellent from the people, civil society and the private sector as well.
At the same time, General Miech Sophanna confirmed that there were 6 types of substandard and illegal counterfeit medicines, including medicines, cosmetics, pesticides, e-cigarettes, consumer products and food. 540 tons.
He said counterfeit products were considered a crime and were linked to transnational crime that was detrimental to the national economy. The Anti-Counterfeiting Committee is not only protecting the well-being of the Cambodian people, but also making investors respect the law and raising taxes for the state.
General Miech Sophanna also commended and thanked the Ministries, partners, institutions and relevant competent forces of the Committee for their cooperation in their efforts to serve the interests of the nation and the people. About 648 km of rural and provincial roads in Banteay Meanchey province were damaged by floods
(Banteay Meanchey): 818 rural roads and provincial roads, equivalent to a length of 648 km, were damaged by floods in the past, which requires urgent repairs for people to travel through and transport goods. This was confirmed by a senior official of the Ministry of Rural Development.
During a visit to Banteay Meanchey province earlier this week, Chrun Theravath, secretary of state for the ministry, said that Banteay Meanchey was one of the provinces affected by the floods. All infrastructures, including roads, were affected and severely damaged, so they had to be rehabilitated after the flood, according to the order of Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
The Secretary of State of the Ministry of Rural Development and Mr. In Chantha, Secretary of State of the Ministry and Director of the Ministry of Disaster Management Secretariat, led a team to inspect the condition of some roads in Svay Chek and Mongkul Borei districts of Banteay Meanchey province affected by the floods.
