Myanmar government retaliates against army chief after accusing it of making "unacceptable mistakes" ahead of elections
RANGON (Reuters) - Myanmar's presidential office on Wednesday responded to criticism of the government's handling of the upcoming general election as biased, with warnings that the army chief's remarks could Cause fear and unrest in the country. This is according to the release by CNA on Wednesday, November 4, 2020.
Min Aung Hlaing, Myanmar's army chief, said in a rare interview on Tuesday that the civilian government was making "unacceptable mistakes" in the upcoming elections. This coming Sunday, Myanmar's second democratic election since the end of strict military rule.
After issuing a second warning of election bias, Commander Min Aung Hlaing told a local newspaper that the military was the country's "guardian" and was closely monitoring election preparations. Keep. He also accused the election commission of violating pre-election laws and procedures.
