(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, on the afternoon of November 3, 2020, received Mr. Peter Ziyato, Minister of Foreign Affairs and Trade of Hungary.
After paying a courtesy call, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also presided over the signing ceremony of three agreements and memoranda of understanding between Cambodia and Hungary. The agreement and memorandum to be signed include:
Peter Ziyato paid a visit to Cambodia this morning, November 3, 2020. Just hours after disembarking, he held a bilateral meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation.
After the meeting, Peter Ziyato and Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon traveled together to open the Hungarian Embassy in Cambodia.
During his visit this morning, Mr. Peter Ziyato also met with Mr. Sous Yara, Member of Parliament, Mr. Veng Sokhon, Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce.
It should be noted that Cambodia and Hungary established diplomatic relations on September 20, 1956. Diplomatic relations between the two countries were severed during the Khmer Rouge regime in 1975.
On this human resource training, three senior Cambodian dignitaries studied in Hungary after the liberation on January 7, 1979, including Mr. Prak Sokhon, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Mr. Long Visalo and Mr. Sar Sovann, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction.
Although Cambodia and Hungary were old friends at a time when Cambodia was in a difficult situation and had long-standing relations, Cambodia and Hungary did not set up an embassy to represent their country.
Only in June 2019, during Samdech Techo Hun Sen's visit to Hungary, the two countries initiated talks to enhance relations and expand cooperation, as well as to establish embassies in each country.
Beginning with Samdech Techo's visit, Haiti led to the visit of Hungarian President Janos Ader to Cambodia at the end of 2019 and the opening of the Hungarian Embassy in Cambodia on November 3, 2020.
Peter Ziyato's current visit to Cambodia comes at a time when the world is struggling with the spread of Kovid 19 and at a time of great economic and social challenges. Because of this, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon considers this visit as a reflection of the strong commitment to further strengthen the friendship and close cooperation between the two countries, Cambodia-Hungary.
Mr. Prak Sokhon also pointed out that Hungary is a close friend of Cambodia. Hungary provided assistance and support to Cambodia at a time when Cambodia was in dire need of assistance after recovering from the flames of the Khmer Rouge regime. Hungarian Blue Hat troops sent to Cambodia under the auspices of the United Nations Transitional Authority in Cambodia (UNTAC).
The Cambodian diplomat also stressed that Hungary's position on the removal of the EBA was another example of a friend who understood the situation in Cambodia and dared to support Cambodia for justice. Peaceful democracy and sustainable development.
Please be reminded that the 2019 report shows that the trade volume between Cambodia and Hungary is about 2 million US dollars. Cambodia exports garments, shoes and rice, while re-importing exercise equipment, wine, whiskey and cosmetics.
Please note that Hungary is a country in the middle of Europe (Central Europe) and has no borders by sea. Hungary is ranked 48th as the most competitive economy in the world.
Hungary's GDP per capita ranks 52nd, with a per capita income of $ 15,923 per year. Hungary's economy, on the other hand, relies heavily on sectors such as mining, electronics, textiles and information technology.
