Minister Vong Soth: Cuban ambassador expresses firm intention to cooperate in social affairs in Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, said that the Cuban Ambassador to Cambodia expressed his intention to cooperate with Cambodia in the field of social affairs to enhance the friendship between the two countries.
In a meeting with the Cambodian Minister of Social Affairs on the occasion of the 60th anniversary of diplomatic relations between the two countries, Ms. Liv Caro Rodriguez said that over the past 60 years, Cuba and Cambodia have always been good friends. Together and achieve many joint achievements.
Regarding the position of the Cuban Embassy, Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs, also expressed his happiness and gratitude for the purpose of the Cuban Embassy. The Minister stated that during the fight against Kovid 19, the Ministry of Social Affairs has many obligations to fulfill, especially the implementation of cash support programs for poor and vulnerable families affected by Kovid 19.
Minister Vong Soth confirmed that this is a big task because this cash support program has facilitated the lives of many poor families, with about 700,000 poor families currently receiving benefits. Regarding the provision of cash, the Royal Government of Cambodia under the leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen also decided to continue to implement the cash support program until the end of 2020.
In addition to the joint education and health sectors, Cuba will seek cooperation in the social and other spheres with Cambodia to strengthen closer ties between the two countries, she said. .
In the same meeting, she also praised Cambodia for measures to prevent the spread of Kovid 19, as well as to help people affected by Kovid 19. She stated that for working and living in Cambodia in the context of Kovid 19 for her family is warm and safe as usual. Minister of Labor: More Cambodian youth and vulnerable groups have decent jobs and benefit from social protection services
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, stated that more and more women, men, youth and vulnerable groups in Cambodia have decent, productive jobs and benefit from transparent and equitable social protection services.
This is the statement made by Minister Ith Sam Heng at the opening ceremony of the National Tripartite Workshop on the Annual Review of the Decent Work Program in Cambodia 2019-2023 held at the Phnom Penh Hotel on the morning of November 3. 2020.
On that occasion, Mr. Ith Sam Heng said that the decent work program in Cambodia 2019-2023 focuses on three main priority goals, including:
1. More and more Cambodian women, men, youth and vulnerable groups are getting decent and productive jobs due to increased economic diversification, productivity, competitiveness and digital economy development.
2. More and more women, men, youth and vulnerable groups in Cambodia have healthy and safe employment and benefit from sustainable, transparent and equitable social protection services.
3. Social, professional relations and labor rights have been improved in line with international labor standards, including for migrant workers.
The tripartite workshop aims to review the key achievements that Cambodia has achieved in 2019 and its priority activities in the coming year, and identify key challenges to promote the successful implementation of the Decent Work Program for Cambodia 2019. - Adopted on March 28, 2019.
At the same time, the Appropriate Work Program for Cambodia 2019-2023 is also an important program to support the participation in achieving these goals, and it is also a framework for cooperation between the Royal Government of Cambodia and social partners to contribute to Achieve Cambodia's national development goals by 2030.
The Minister said that the International Labor Organization (ILO) has always supported the development of Cambodia through the implementation of policies and programs, as well as appropriate employment programs for Cambodia, which is necessary to ensure measurable progress at the level. The country aims to achieve decent employment goals for women, men, youth and vulnerable groups.
