Fake Monk Detained in Chamkarmon District
(Phnom Penh): A man pretended to be a monk and walked around the wrong place at the wrong time, so the villagers complained to the police and called the police station for questioning. Finally, they found out that the monk was fake and arrested him at 2 o'clock. In the afternoon of November 2, 2020, in front of house No. 60, Village 1, Mao Tse Toung Street, Sangkat Boeung Trabek, Khan Chamkar Morn.
The impostor, a 30-year-old monk named In Tep, alias Say, whose occupation and residence are not known, was born in Sangkat Doeum Kor, Khan Toul Kork.
Police received information from residents that a man suspected of pretending to be a monk was dressed and walking around people's houses, saying that there was a flower festival at a distant pagoda and that there was a lack of money for construction.
The cult official of Chamkar Mon district confirmed that after interrogation, they found out that the man was a fake monk, not a monk with a proper pagoda, even without his own place of residence.
After the arrest, the officers educated and took off their clothes to wear normal clothes and advised them to stop committing acts of destruction of Buddhism and to change into good people.
Mr. Hong Chantheng, Chief of Boeung Trabek Administration Post, said that after the officers were educated, they were sent to the Chamkar Mon District Inspectorate for legal action. Khmer Silk Research Center is experimenting with artificial food for her worms to reduce costs (Video inside)
(Phnom Penh): The Khmer Silk Research Center, located at the Royal University of Phnom Penh, the first silk center in Cambodia, has been experimenting with the production of artificial feed for her larvae to reduce the cost of farmers.
In addition, Dr. Mey Kalyan said that when the artificial feed for her worms is successful, it will facilitate many of the work of the breeder, including the cost, because this feed can feed her worms. Up to 12 days.
Dr. Mey Kalyan added that artificial food for her worms in Japan has been produced for a long time, while Thailand is experimenting like in Cambodia.
Dr. Mey Kalyan pointed out that the Khmer Silk Research Center is currently experimenting with artificial food for her worms better before deciding to produce and distribute to people.
* The first goal of the center is to research and produce healthy larvae that can produce long and high quality silk fibers and distribute them to farmers through selected pole farmers.
* The second goal is to research the origin of her worms and silk to produce products such as soaps, cosmetics and supplements.
According to Dr. Mey Kanyan, Cambodia has now laid the foundation for the silk industry, with a clear institution to research the production of healthy larvae and acceptable silk yields.
Dr. continued that for the production of her worms so far, the Khmer Silk Research Center can produce up to more than 20 varieties and research Mon species as well.
At the same time, in order to revitalize Cambodia's silk sector, Dr. called on all stakeholders to join together to increase the production of silk to meet domestic demand, reduce imports from abroad, while in one year Cambodia Imported up to 400 tons of silk.
