A Singaporean's attempt to divide ASEAN will not succeed
(Laos): ASEAN citizens, especially citizens from recent member states, including the Lao People's Democratic Republic and the Kingdom of Cambodia, are shocked to learn that a respected defender of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) has decided Stop defending ASEAN and send a confusing message to the world, saying that Laos and Cambodia should be expelled from ASEAN.
After the difficult independence, the Lao People's Democratic Republic and its people are well aware that independence, peace and development cooperation are very valuable.
In the last two decades, as a member of ASEAN, Laos has made great strides in socio-economic development, although the initial development was still low due to the devastating war. During that time, Laos contributed to ASEAN in many ways.
First, Laos' domestic political stability is important in contributing to regional peace and stability.
Third, Laos is not only a good host for ASEAN and other relevant meetings, but also a major contributor to the ASEAN Charter and other documents. Laos also successfully hosted the Asia-Europe Meeting (ASEM) in 2012.
Laos also hosted bilateral talks between the Foreign Ministers of the Republic of Korea and the Democratic People's Republic of Korea when Laos chaired ASEAN in 2004. These are just a few examples to remind the Koscian Ambassador.
Our final message is that Laos is an independent and sovereign state. Laos is well aware that we need to not only promote our national interests but also our regional interests. Laos is a good and responsible member of the United Nations, ASEAN, the World Trade Organization and many other international institutions. These are law-abiding organizations that Laos adheres to.
Laos and other ASEAN member countries have each contributed to the promotion and maintenance of peace and development in the region and beyond. Laos has great potential to create more opportunities and benefits for ASEAN when those achievements and strengths are combined with other ASEAN member states.
As a former diplomat, the Cox's Bazar ambassador should understand this issue and promote ASEAN unity rather than dividing ASEAN, which seriously affects the interests of individual ASEAN member states and ASEAN as a whole.
