Minister Veng Sakhon expressed his personal view that in recent years, the agricultural sector has developed remarkably, and this growth is in line with the context of economic growth, population growth, product demand trends. Agriculture with quantity and quality in both domestic and foreign markets.
In fact, the vegetable production sector, farmers began to grow indoors, stop carrying water, irrigate vegetables, use drip irrigation system or irrigate vegetables instead, stop using hoe, but use machinery, use fertilizers and pesticides in a technical and safe level, both health products Producers, consumers and farmers use the GAP system to assess the quality of production, compile community production clusters and implement contract farming, etc.
As an additional source of knowledge, experience, and strong stance while staying in Israel, Minister Veng Sakhon has given some good tips for students to consider and implement properly to achieve good results, including: Sunday:
* First: To study hard, learn and absorb experience, knowledge, skills, both technical and management to strengthen their capacity on modern technical skills.
* Second, must respect the laws, rules, traditions, culture and customs of the country, diligently and maintain the national culture, traditions and customs of Cambodia to be consistent. Pay close attention to personal health and job safety, have good relationships in the family and school.
* Third: There must be good cooperation with all international students who live, study and work together.
* Fourth: You have to take a good opportunity to absorb both knowledge, skills and experience to capitalize on developing yourself to become a person capable enough to use or apply new technologies in the future.
* Fifth, RUA should organize a program to exchange professors and teachers between Cambodia and Israel to get new technologies that are simple, easy for farmers to implement, reduce labor difficulties, time and economic efficiency.
According to a report by Ngor Ponthan, Rector of the Royal University of Agriculture, the Israeli internship program (RUA) has been cooperating with Agrostudies on agricultural human resource training since 2008 with the aim of promoting higher education. Theory and practice for students for 11 months, and this cooperation is 13 years old.
