(Phnom Penh): When the accused is sentenced by the court to imprisonment, the accused will be called a "prisoner". Inmates who are incarcerated in prisons are deprived of a large part of their freedoms, but despite such deprivation of freedoms, inmates still enjoy certain rights in prisons.
To better understand what are the rights of prisoners in prisons? For this week, FRESH NEWS's Legal Awareness Program, in collaboration with the Cambodian Bar Association for International Cooperation, would like to raise the topic of "Prisoners' Rights in Prisons" to the following students:
When inmates are incarcerated in prisons, inmates are provided with uniforms and adequate supplies in their cells according to the weather conditions for use during their imprisonment. Keep in good condition and change if necessary.
As for food, all prisoners receive nutritious food that can maintain good health and body, as well as clean water for consumption. If it is a national holiday such as New Year's Day, January 7, "Victory Day over the genocidal regime", Khmer New Year and Pchum Ben, all prisoners' diets will be added one or two more than usual.
In addition, inmates may receive food from outside the prison purchased from their own budget or provided by a visitor, but the food must be inspected and approved by prison officials before being handed over to the inmate.
In short, although inmates are largely deprived of their liberty, inmates are given certain rights in relation to dress, nutrition, health care, education, and vocational training, in order to rehabilitate. Which is for the benefit of prisoners to build themselves into good children, good friends and good citizens in the future.
