More than 5,000 hectares of rain-fed rice fields affected by floods and more than 2,000 hectares damaged in Preah Vihear province, the Ministry of Agriculture inspected the damage assessment to rehabilitate the people
(Preah Vihear): The flood situation in Stung Sen and rain affected the rain-fed rice area of people in Preah Vihear province in 8 districts, totaling 5,023 hectares, equal to 3145 families and rain-fed land of 6 farmers. 2,776 hectares, equivalent to 2,096 families, were damaged.
According to the report of the working group led by Mr. Kuy Marindy, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, representing Minister Veng Sakhon, accompanied by Mr. Peng Trida, Director of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Agriculture, said that in this province, people grow vegetables and livestock. Some also suffered casualties and subsequent deaths.
In order to rehabilitate and address this challenge, the working group, with the approval of Mr. Kuy Marindy, requested the Minister's decision to provide short-term vegetable seeds for 2096 damaged farmers to apply for livestock vaccinated. 3,000 doses of crab, 200 bottles of antiperspirant, 200 bottles of antibiotics, request for light and medium rice varieties for the 2021 rainy season for 2096 families, equal to 20960 tons.
(Siem Reap): Some residents in Angkor Chum district, Siem Reap province, have asked the relevant authorities and departments to help stop the traffic of heavy vehicles, some of which are suspected of being overweight, which has caused serious damage to the road. A paved road connecting National Road 6 (Puok Market point) to the roundabout in Angkor Chum district.
Residents living along the road said that since the road was completed in early 2020, people living in Angkor Chum and Puok districts are very happy. But lately, this road has been damaged by heavy trucks carrying steel, cement, sand and overweight rice, escaping public scales and being heavily trafficked.
Residents in this area are very angry about the destruction by companies and transport traders without any restraint or indifference from the authorities or commune authorities. People are worried about this road, if not prevented in time, this road will be more and more damaged in the near future and cause a lot of loss to the state budget.
Residents have asked the authorities to help protect the road and prevent heavy vehicles from driving on this road so that people can use this road for a long time to come.
Regarding this case, Mr. Kheng Nhol, Director of the Department of Rural Development of Siem Reap Province, confirmed to Fresh News that he had already informed the district authorities and inspected the condition of this road, looking for a solution for the people. He claimed that the road was indeed damaged by heavy vehicles and some were damaged by floods.
He claims that the cars have cages where the percentage of overweight is likely to be high, however, there are no scales in the area. He hopes to be able to settle after the holidays as well.
