(Phnom Penh): Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance, has stated that the new draft law on investment in the Kingdom of Cambodia is scheduled to be finalized by the end of November 2020 so that it can contribute to accelerating the recovery. Recovering Cambodia's potential and economic growth, attracting foreign direct investment to Cambodia, increasing the growth opportunities of the private sector in the context of Kovid-19 disease.
As pointed out at the 18th Royal Government-Private Sector Forum, the "New Draft Law on Investment of the Kingdom of Cambodia" was born out of the Royal Government's careful consideration under the wise leadership of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth emphasized that the "new draft law" takes into account the openness, favorableness and attractiveness of investment, creating a competitive environment, especially the protection of rights and interests. Investor Law in the Kingdom of Cambodia.
The private sector appreciated the efforts of the Joint Working Group to prepare the "new draft law" and provided some comments and suggestions on the new draft law. Any questions or concerns and input raised by the private sector are carefully considered in the preparation of this "new draft law".
In order to continue the procedure for preparing the revised draft law, the Meeting requested the private sector to continue to conduct in-depth and comprehensive review and continue to provide additional feedback to the Joint Working Group on the preparation of the "new draft law on investment in Kingdom of Cambodia ”with the participation of stakeholders both in spirit and content.
The Deputy Prime Minister also praised and appreciated all stakeholders, especially the private sector, for their active participation and good cooperation in preparing the "new draft law".
Rescuers are focusing on three villages in the central part of the country where landslides triggered by Typhoon Molave have killed at least 19 people. More than 40 others are suspected of being buried in the mud.
